@startuml
title I2C bus - transaction layer behaviour (docs/transaction_layer.md)
!pragma teoz true
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign direction

participant "Bus participant A" as A
participant "I2C bus (SDA + SCL)" as BUS
participant "Bus participant B" as B

note over A,B
Target role stays enabled, including during controller operation.
The controller's flush budget F limits how long it keeps trying: queue head,
bus waits, backoff and retries. F never cuts an attempt on the bus: a controller
that has sent START ends its transaction with STOP. Only the hold limits
(controller B_hold, target S_max) bound an attempt.
ACK/NAK follows each complete byte, including during arbitration;
individual ACK/NAK clocks are omitted below.
end note

== Arbitration lost (ARLO) ==
A -> BUS: START
& B -> BUS: Concurrent START
note over A,B: Matching bits do not indicate that arbitration has been won.
loop Each transmitted bit until B loses arbitration
 A -> BUS: Drive 0 / release for 1; sample SDA
 & B -> BUS: Drive 0 / release for 1; sample SDA
 opt B transmits 1 but samples 0
  B -> B: ARLO; cease controller driving; no STOP
 end
end
B -> B: Clear ARLO; request START for retry immediately
note over BUS,B: Peripheral waits until bus available.
alt B's flush timer not expired when bus becomes available
 A <--> BUS: Transfer remaining bits
 A -> BUS: STOP
 B -> BUS: Pending START
 B <--> BUS: Transfer bits; arbitration remains possible
 B -> BUS: STOP
 note over B: Once B has sent START, F no longer ends the attempt.
else B's flush timer expires while waiting
 A <--> BUS: Transfer bits
 & B -> B: Withdraw pending START; report Timeout (last: ArbitrationLost)
 note over A,B: A's ongoing transfer is unaffected.
end

== Bus busy ==
A -> BUS: START
B -> B: Request START
note over BUS,B: Peripheral waits until bus available.
alt B's flush timer not expired when bus becomes available
 A <--> BUS: Transfer remaining bits
 A -> BUS: STOP
 B -> BUS: Pending START
 B <--> BUS: Transfer bits; arbitration remains possible
 B -> BUS: STOP
 note over B: Once B has sent START, F no longer ends the attempt.
else B's flush timer expires while waiting
 A <--> BUS: Transfer bits
 & B -> B: Withdraw pending START; report Timeout (last: Busy)
 note over A,B: A's ongoing transfer is unaffected.
end

== Address NAK ==
A -> BUS: START; target address + direction
BUS -> A: Address NAK
A -> BUS: STOP
note over A,B
Possible causes: boot/reset, target not listening at this address,
or target temporarily unavailable (e.g. busy with a long internal operation).
Address NAK alone does not identify the cause.
Retrying alternate addresses is an open extension (transaction_layer.md 4.2).
end note
loop Retry the same address until address ACK or flush timer expires
 A -> A: Wait backoff t_backoff
 alt Flush timer expired
  A -> A: Discard transfer; report Timeout (last: AddrNak)
 else Flush timer not expired
  A -> A: Request START
  alt Flush timer expires while waiting for the bus
   A -> A: Withdraw pending START; report Timeout (last: AddrNak)
  else START sent
   A -> BUS: START; same target address + direction
   BUS -> A: Address ACK / NAK
   opt Address NAK
    A -> BUS: STOP
   end
  end
 end
end
opt Address ACK received
 note over A: From here on F has no effect on the attempt.
 alt Transfer completes
  A <--> BUS: Transfer remaining bits
  A -> BUS: STOP
  A -> A: Report Success
 else Transfer error
  A -> A: Terminate transfer; report the error
 else Hold limit B_hold runs out
  A <--> BUS: Transfer bits until the next byte boundary
  A -> BUS: Forced STOP (read: NACK the next byte first)
  A -> A: Report Timeout
 end
end

== Data NAK ==
A -> BUS: START; target address + write direction
BUS -> A: Address ACK
A -> BUS: Data byte(s)
BUS -> A: Data NAK
A -> BUS: STOP
A -> A: Report DataNak (final)
note over A,B
The target has already received the acknowledged bytes and may have acted on
them. The controller does not resend automatically; the caller decides.
end note

== Clock stretching ==
note over A,B: A acts as controller; B is the addressed target.
B -> BUS: Hold SCL low
A -> BUS: Release SCL for next high phase
BUS -> A: SCL stays low; peripheral waits
note over A
A cannot generate STOP while B holds SCL low.
A keeps the transaction and waits; its flush timer F does not end it.
end note
note over B
B's stretch limit S_max counts only the time B holds SCL itself,
not the time SCL is low: A may hold SCL low at the same time.
It is independent of B's own controller flush timer.
end note
alt B ready within S_max
 B -> BUS: Release SCL
 BUS -> A: SCL rises; peripheral resumes transfer
else B not ready within S_max
 B -> BUS: Release SCL and SDA
 B -> B: Abandon target transfer; ignore bus until STOP or START
 BUS -> A: SCL rises; peripheral resumes transfer
 alt Write
  BUS -> A: Data NAK
  A -> BUS: STOP
  A -> A: Report DataNak
 else Read
  BUS -> A: 0xFF for the remaining bytes
  A -> BUS: NACK last byte; STOP
  A -> A: Report Success (data invalid; detectable only by a payload check)
 end
else SCL low for t_stuck (B does not release, e.g. hung firmware)
 A -> A: Report bus failure
 note over A,B
 Recovery or failure mode per transaction_layer.md section 7.
 A still owes the STOP: as soon as SCL is released again
 (e.g. after B was reset), A sends STOP before any other access.
 end note
end

== Switching targets ==
hnote across
Layer policy: STOP between different targets, then a new START.
No repeated START when switching targets.
end hnote
@enduml
